course: dictionary
lesson: 12950
title: Excess: more than is needed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Excess means an amount that is more than is needed, allowed or usual. The word often shows up when something goes past a limit. «The bag was 5 kilograms over the limit, so Sam had to pay for excess baggage.» The bag had extra weight beyond what was allowed, so the extra is the excess.
explain: You can spot excess by looking for a limit. «If the limit is 20 and you have 25, the excess is 5.» The limit is the line you must not cross, and the excess is what goes over it. Quick check: can you find the number or rule that sets the limit?
explain: Excess also appears in phrases. «The crowd was in excess of 1,000 people» means more than 1,000 people. «Mum poured off the excess fat from the pan» means she removed the extra fat that was not needed. Ask: is there more here than it needs to be?
fact: Excess means more than is needed, allowed or usual.
fact: «In excess of» means more than, as in «in excess of 1,000 people».
fact: Excess can be a noun, as in «an excess of rain», or an adjective, as in «excess baggage».
fact: In maths, the excess is the amount over a limit: if the limit is 20 and you have 25, the excess is 5.
fact: In the UK and Australia, excess can also mean the amount you pay yourself in an insurance claim before the insurer pays the rest.
exercise: «Mum poured off the excess fat from the pan.» What does excess mean here? | The cooking time | The pan | *The extra fat, more than was needed | The oven
exercise: If the limit is 20 and you have 25, what is the excess? | *5 | 45 | 20 | 25
exercise: Find the phrase that means more than. | in the middle of | at the end of | by the sea | *in excess of
test: «The airline charged her for excess baggage.» What does excess baggage mean? | Baggage with wheels | *Baggage that is more than the allowed weight or amount | Baggage that is missing | Baggage that is light and empty
test: In «excess baggage», what kind of word is excess? | Verb | *Adjective | Adverb | Noun
exam: Read this: «The rain was in excess of 50 millimetres.» What does in excess of mean? | *More than 50 millimetres | Exactly 50 millimetres | Less than 50 millimetres | Under the rain
